%\section{Homework 2}

%\section{IVs}

\begin{frame}{Motivation}
We discussed different sources of \alert{endogeneity}, i.e. a violation of MLR.4: $E[u_i | \boldsymbol{X}_i] \neq 0$

Instrumental Variable (IV) estimation is an approach to get a consistent estimator if $u_i$ and $\boldsymbol{X}_i$ are correlated.
\pause

Terminology: we call a variable endogenous [\color{blue}exogenous\color{black}] if it is correlated [\color{blue}not correlated\color{black}] with the error term.

\pause
Idea: Find a variable (the instrument) that is \textbf{correlated with the endogenous regressor} (say, $X_{1i}$), \textbf{but uncorrelated with $u_i$} \\
$\rightarrow$ decompose the variation in $X_{1i}$ into an endogenous and an exogenous part \\
$\rightarrow$ extract the part that is not correlated with $u_i$, and exploit only that part in the estimation of $\boldsymbol{\beta}$
\end{frame}


\begin{frame}{Illustration: Instrumental Variables (IV) approach}

\begin{columns}
